1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:03,984
Maya sees two hundred thousand dollars on a dashboard and

2
00:00:04,007 --> 00:00:06,819
a one hundred fifty thousand dollar approved agreement.

3
00:00:07,126 --> 00:00:09,976
This is a fresh fictional discrepancy case.

4
00:00:10,322 --> 00:00:13,801
Do not immediately overwrite a value merely because the two

5
00:00:13,801 --> 00:00:14,680
numbers differ.

6
00:00:15,738 --> 00:00:20,120
Is the report showing potential pipeline, signed bookings, or recognized

7
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:20,715
revenue?

8
00:00:21,035 --> 00:00:23,370
Which period and which records should count?

9
00:00:23,702 --> 00:00:27,017
The original proposed amount may be a valid historical value

10
00:00:27,053 --> 00:00:29,542
while being wrong for a current bookings report.

11
00:00:30,605 --> 00:00:35,414
Where history tracking is configured and available, inspect relevant changes

12
00:00:35,414 --> 00:00:36,451
and their origin.

13
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:41,511
Compare the revised quote, approval state, signed agreement reference, and

14
00:00:41,511 --> 00:00:42,063
order.

15
00:00:42,366 --> 00:00:45,911
The evidence may reveal an old proposal amount left on

16
00:00:45,911 --> 00:00:46,795
the opportunity.

17
00:00:47,837 --> 00:00:51,497
Did the business omit a synchronization rule, or did an

18
00:00:51,497 --> 00:00:53,098
existing integration fail?

19
00:00:53,417 --> 00:00:57,428
Check configuration and authoritative definitions with the owners.

20
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:02,038
Search for other affected opportunities so the visible discrepancy does

21
00:01:02,038 --> 00:01:04,786
not hide a broader reporting problem.

22
00:01:05,828 --> 00:01:09,320
The right correction might be a record update, report filter,

23
00:01:09,695 --> 00:01:12,236
mapping repair, or workflow change.

24
00:01:12,547 --> 00:01:16,976
Reconcile the immediate report and validate the proposed lasting fix.

25
00:01:17,286 --> 00:01:21,297
A correction should follow evidence and ownership, not an analyst’s

26
00:01:21,297 --> 00:01:21,885
guess.

27
00:01:22,938 --> 00:01:26,379
Tell Maya what differs, what the records support, how much

28
00:01:26,379 --> 00:01:29,526
is affected, and what decision or action comes next.

29
00:01:29,832 --> 00:01:34,706
A data dictionary then records definitions, sources, owners, and usage

30
00:01:34,884 --> 00:01:37,864
so future reports speak the same language.
